mdhn:Staff_becomes_Serpent a mdhn:NarrativeEpisode ;
    rdfs:label "Aaron’s staff becomes a serpent before Pharaoh"@en ,
               "عصای هارون به اژدها / مار تبدیل می‌شود"@fa ,
               "Moses’ staff becomes a snake"@en ;    
    rdfs:comment """When Moses and Aaron appear before Pharaoh, Aaron casts down his staff 
    and it becomes a serpent (or tannīn / sea-monster / dragon in some traditions and translations). 
    The Egyptian magicians perform the same feat with their staffs, but Aaron’s serpent 
    swallows theirs (Exodus 7:8–12). 
    The scene is a key demonstration of divine power over Egyptian magic and appears 
    in both Biblical and Islamic prophetic cycles."""@en ;    
    mdhn:icWikiDataURL "" ;    
    mdhn:charactersInvolved mdhn:Moses ,
                            mdhn:Harun ,
                            mdhn:Pharaoh ;    
    mdhn:isPartOf mdhn:Biblical_Stories, mdhn:Story_of_Moses, mdhn:Qisas_al_Anbiya;
    skos:exactMatch   mdhn:iconclass71E1164 ;
    skos:relatedMatch mdhn:iconclass71E11641 ;
    skos:relatedMatch wd:Q9077 .                  # Moses    

mdhn:Moses_and_the_Seventy_Elders a mdhn:NarrativeEpisode ;
    rdfs:label "Moses and the Seventy Elders hearing the voice of God"@en ,
               "موسی و هفتاد عالم در حال شنیدن ندای خداوند"@fa ;
    
    rdfs:comment """Scene in which Moses (Musa), accompanied by seventy elders (or learned men), 
    hears the voice of God speaking from the clouds. Moses is typically shown elevated on a rock 
    or mountain, often wearing a prophetic veil, while the seventy lie prostrate below him. 
    Mountains form the background. 
    The episode appears in both Biblical tradition (Exodus 24 and Numbers 11) and Islamic 
    narrative sources, and is notably illustrated in the Jāmiʿ al-Tawārīkh of Rashīd al-Dīn."""@en ;    
    mdhn:icWikiDataURL "" ;   # no direct Wikidata item for this specific episode    
    mdhn:charactersInvolved mdhn:Moses ;
    mdhn:isPartOf mdhn:Story_of_Moses, mdhn:Qisas_al_Anbiya ;          # or a more specific Moses cycle if you create one    
    skos:exactMatch   mdhn:iconclass71E1_MosesSeventyElders ;
    skos:relatedMatch mdhn:iconclass71E1 ;
    skos:relatedMatch wd:Q9077 .  

mdhn:Finding_of_Moses a mdhn:NarrativeEpisode ;
    rdfs:label "Finding of Moses"@en ,
               "یافتن موسی در رود نیل"@fa ,
               "The Finding of Moses / Moses in the Bulrushes"@en ;    
    rdfs:comment """The infant Moses is discovered floating in a basket on the River Nile 
    by women of the Egyptian royal household. Pharaoh had ordered the killing of newborn 
    Hebrew boys. Moses’ mother placed him in the river; he was rescued and adopted by 
    Pharaoh’s daughter (often identified as Queen Bithia / Bithiah), shown with long dark 
    hair. One of the accompanying women, of lighter complexion, is usually the Israelite 
    nurse (Moses’ own mother or sister). 
    The scene is common in both Biblical and Islamic manuscript traditions."""@en ;    
    mdhn:icWikiDataURL "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q19897958" ;  # or leave empty if preferred    
    mdhn:charactersInvolved mdhn:Moses ,
                            mdhn:Pharaohs_Daughter ;   # create later if needed    
    mdhn:isPartOf mdhn:Story_of_Moses, mdhn:Qisas_al_Anbiya;
    skos:exactMatch   mdhn:iconclass71E1124 ;
    skos:relatedMatch mdhn:iconclass71E1 ;
    skos:relatedMatch wd:Q9077 .   

mdhn:Jonah_under_the_Gourd a mdhn:NarrativeEpisode ;
    rdfs:label "Jonah under the Gourd Plant"@en ,
               "یونس در دهان ماهی زیر بوته کدو"@fa ,
               "Yunus and the Gourd"@en ;    
    rdfs:comment """After being cast up by the great fish (whale), the prophet Jonah (Yunus) 
    lies in the shade of a gourd plant that God caused to grow overnight to provide him with 
    food and shelter (Jonah 4:6). 
    The reclining pose of Jonah under the plant is a motif already present in early Christian art. 
    In many manuscript traditions the whale is depicted as a large fish, reflecting limited 
    knowledge of the actual creature. 
    The scene is part of both Biblical and Islamic prophetic cycles."""@en ;    
    mdhn:icWikiDataURL "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2468262" ;   # no single stable Wikidata item for this specific episode    
    mdhn:charactersInvolved mdhn:Jonah ;    
    mdhn:isPartOf mdhn:Biblical_Stories, mdhn:Qisas_al_Anbiya;
    skos:exactMatch   mdhn:iconclass71V42 ;
    skos:relatedMatch mdhn:iconclass71V ;
    skos:relatedMatch wd:Q2468262 .                  # Jonah        

