mdhn:Story_of_Seven_sleepers_of_Ephesus a mdhn:NarrativeEpisode ;
    rdfs:label "Seven Sleepers of Ephesus"@en ,
               "اصحاب کهف"@fa ,
               "Aṣḥāb al-Kahf"@ar ,
               "Seven Sleepers"@en ;    
    rdfs:comment """Christian and Islamic legend of seven (or more) young believers 
    who fled the persecution of the Roman emperor Decius (Digyanus) and were walled up 
    in a cave where they fell into a miraculous sleep. 
    They awoke during the reign of Theodosius II (c. AD 450). 
    In the Qur’an (Sūrat al-Kahf 18:9–26) they are praised for their faith; 
    the period of their sleep is given as 309 years. 
    Muslim tradition sometimes locates the cave in Toyug (Central Asia) rather than Ephesus. 
    The scene is frequently illustrated in both Christian and Islamic manuscript painting."""@en ;
    mdhn:saidToBeTheSameAs wd:Q4071833;    
    mdhn:icWikiDataURL "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q466973" ;      
    mdhn:isPartOf mdhn:Biblical_Stories, mdhn:Islamic_Stories ;
    skos:exactMatch   mdhn:iconclass11H_SevenSleepers ;
    skos:relatedMatch mdhn:iconclass_Ashab_al_Kahf ;
    skos:relatedMatch wd:Q466973 .

mdhn:Jurjis_led_to_the_King_of_Mosul a mdhn:NarrativeEpisode ;
    rdfs:label "Jurjis led to the King of Mosul"@en ,
               "جرجیس در حال برده‌شدن به نزد پادشاه موصل"@fa ,
               "Jirjis before the King of Mosul"@en ;    
    rdfs:comment """The Prophet Jurjis (Jirjis / Saint George in Islamic tradition) 
    is shown with a chain around his neck, being led to the King of Mosul for refusing 
    to worship idols. According to Islamic sources, his steadfast faith caused him to be 
    miraculously protected from the successive tortures that followed. 
    The episode forms part of the Islamic prophetic cycle and is illustrated in the 
    Jāmiʿ al-Tawārīkh of Rashīd al-Dīn."""@en ;    
    mdhn:icWikiDataURL "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q48438" ;  # Saint George (closest stable identity)    
    mdhn:charactersInvolved mdhn:Saint_George ;    
    mdhn:isPartOf mdhn:Biblical_Stories, mdhn:Islamic_Stories ;
    skos:exactMatch   mdhn:iconclass_Jurjis_led_to_King ;
    skos:relatedMatch mdhn:iconclass11H_George ;
    skos:relatedMatch wd:Q48438 .    


mdhn:Birth_of_the_Prophet_Muhammad a mdhn:NarrativeEpisode ;
    rdfs:label "Birth of the Prophet Muhammad"@en ,
               "تولد پیامبر محمد"@fa ,
               "Mawlid al-Nabī"@ar ;    
    rdfs:comment """The birth of the Prophet Muhammad. 
    The mother (Āmina) is shown reclining on a couch in the centre of the scene, 
    while the newborn child is handed to one of two angels who have witnessed the event 
    (visible on the left of the composition). 
    This visual formula appears in the Jāmiʿ al-Tawārīkh of Rashīd al-Dīn and later 
    prophetic cycles; it adapts compositional elements familiar from Christian nativity 
    iconography while remaining within the Islamic narrative tradition."""@en ;    
    mdhn:icWikiDataURL "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q9458" ;  # Muhammad (person)    
    mdhn:charactersInvolved mdhn:Muhammad ,mdhn:Aminah ;    
    mdhn:isPartOf mdhn:Islamic_Stories ;    
    skos:exactMatch   mdhn:iconclass_Birth_of_Muhammad ;
    skos:relatedMatch wd:Q9458 .    

